lation-to-reality gaps and significant physiological variabilities. Limited interpretability for their outputs is another major hurdle towards their adoption as a DT for healthcare decision-making purposes. Because of these bottlenecks, these blackbox neural DTs are being actively developed but further away from translational research compared to their mechanistic counterparts, in the domain of cardiac EP [ 19 , 28 , 20 , 54 , 29 ] . Gray-box physics-informed models: To bridge the gap between the two ends of the spectrum, physics-informed learning has taken on a variety of forms among which the most well-known may be the physics-informed neural networks (PINNs) [ 35 ] . In this type of gray-box models , a neural-network ℳ ϕ \mathcal{M}_{\phi} with weight parameters ϕ \phi , is supervised by a partial differential equation (PDE) ℳ PHY \mathcal{M}_{	ext{PHY}} with known mathematical expressions and parameter θ 	heta . When θ 	heta is known, the PINN can be interpreted as an alternative way of solving the PDE without traditional numerical methods. When θ 	heta is unknown, the PINN can be optimized at the same time as θ 	heta , delivering a personalized PINN with limited data. By leveraging the known physics in its loss functions, the PINN reduces the requirement of large training data compared to its black-box counterpart. ℳ ϕ \mathcal{M}_{\phi} , however, essentially remains a black-box model that faces similar challenges of generalization and interpretability as a DT. Furthermore, it will inherit any assumptions, uncertainties, and knowledge gaps in the ℳ PHY \mathcal{M}_{	ext{PHY}} used to govern its training. Similarly, PINN-based DTs represents an emerging line of research that has not yet reached translational studies in the domain of cardiac EP [ 16 , 50 , 37 , 43 ] . 2.2.2 DT Optimization with Patient-Specific Measurements Existing approaches for optimizing the white-, black-, or gray-box DTs can be loosely categorized into two groups. Importantly, here we focus on the adaptation ( i.e. , personalization) of the functional parameters of a DT to patient-specific measurements. We do not include reviews of works on the initial construction of these DTs, including initial construction of a mechanistic EP model from patient-specific imaging data [ 44 , 32 , 2 , 53 ] , or the offline training of a neural surrogate for such EP models [ 20 , 28 , 54 , 29 , 25 ] . Per-instance inference: In most approaches, traditional numerical optimization strategies are employed to optimize the parameters of the DT to specific measurements from a specific instance, known interchangeably as personalization , calibration , or adaptation in different works. These apply to the optimization of most mechanistic and PINN models [ 14 , 6 , 17 , 16 , 50 ] , while some recent works also considered per-instance optimization of the input mechanistic parameters θ 	heta to a black-box neural surrogate ℳ ϕ ​ ( θ ) \mathcal{M}_{\phi}(	heta) to fit observed patient-specific data [ 29 , 38 ] . This per-instance inference can be generally expressed as: θ ^ = arg ⁡ min θ ⁡ ‖ g ⁡ ( ℳ PHY / ϕ ​ ( θ ) ) − 𝐲 o ​ b ​ s ‖ 2 2 \hat{	heta}=\arg\min_{	heta}||g(\mathcal{M}_{	extrm{PHY}/\phi}(\mathbf{	heta}))-\mathbf{y}_{obs}||_{2}^{2} (2) for a mechanistic DT ℳ PHY \mathcal{M}_{	ext{PHY}} or neural DT ℳ ϕ \mathcal{M}_{\phi} . In the setting of PINNs, both ϕ \phi and θ 	heta are simultaneously optimized: { ϕ ^ , θ ^ } = arg ⁡ min ϕ , θ ​ { ‖ ℳ ϕ − 𝐲 o ​ b ​ s ‖ 2 2 + λ ​ ‖ ℳ PHY ​ ( ℳ ϕ , θ ) ‖ 2 2 } \{\hat{\phi},\hat{	heta}\}=\arg\min_{\phi,	heta}\{||\mathcal{M}_{\phi}-\mathbf{y}_{obs}||_{2}^{2}+\lambda||\mathcal{M}_{	ext{PHY}}(\mathcal{M}_{\phi};	heta)||_{2}^{2}\} (3) where the first term fits ℳ ϕ \mathcal{M}_{\phi} ’s output to available data (data-fitting loss), and the second term encourages ℳ ϕ \mathcal{M}_{\phi} ’s output to follow the governing PDE specified by ℳ PHY \mathcal{M}_{	ext{PHY}} with unknown physics parameter θ 	heta (PDE residual loss). Regardless of the underlying models, per-instance inference involves iterative optimization for the purpose of DT adaptation, limiting their ability to rapidly adapt to limited live data. Amortized reconstructive-inference: Alternatively, amortized inference represents a learning-based strategy to train a neural network (often known as the inference or encoder network) to infer the parameter of a DT model from available training data. Once trained, this inference network can replace iterative per-instance optimization with rapid feedforward computation, realizing rapid adaptation. In analogy to the per-instance optimization objective in Equation ( 2 ), the amortized optimization objective can be expressed as: { ω ^ , ϕ ^ } = arg min ω , ϕ | | g ( ℳ PHY / ϕ ( ℰ ω ( 𝐲 o ​ b ​ s ) ) − 𝐲 o ​ b ​ s | | 2 2 \{\hat{\omega},\hat{\phi}\}=\arg\min_{\omega,\phi}||g(\mathcal{M}_{	extrm{PHY}/\phi}(\mathcal{E}_{\omega}(\mathbf{y}_{obs}))-\mathbf{y}_{obs}||_{2}^{2} (4) where ℰ ω ​ ( 𝐲 o ​ b ​ s ) \mathcal{E}_{\omega}(\mathbf{y}_{obs}) is the inference network parameterized by ω \omega . Note that the estimated parameter θ 	heta is extracted by ℰ ω ​ ( 𝐲 o ​ b ​ s ) \mathcal{E}_{\omega}(\mathbf{y}_{obs}) from observed 𝐲 o ​ b ​ s \mathbf{y}_{obs} , and the adapted DT is asked to reconstruct 𝐲 o ​ b ​ s \mathbf{y}_{obs} with the estimated θ 	heta . This is why we refer to this strategy as amortized reconstructive-inference . While a common concept in latent variable models such as variational auto-encoders [ 22 ] , this strategy has only recently begun to appear in DT adaptation. In cardiac EP, encoder-style amortized adaptation has been explored in neural surrogate settings [ 19 ] , whereas its use for mechanistic or hybrid DT adaptation remains comparatively limited [ 43 ] . Types of measurements: Another relevant point is the type of measurements used to calibrate a DT. In the current literature of cardiac EP, it is common to use indirect measures, such as noninvasive body-surface electrocardiograms (ECGs) or minimally-invasive heart-surface electrograms (EGMs), to calibrate a white-box DT [ 14 , 6 , 17 ] . For gray- or black-box DTs that have data-driven components, however, most existing works assume direct supervision on the variables ( e.g., action potential) being modeled [ 16 , 50 , 43 , 19 , 28 , 20 , 52 ] : as aforementioned, this assumption is often not applicable in cardiac DTs, e.g., the spatiotemporal propagation of action potentials in the heart is only partially or indirectly observed. 2.2.3 Summary of Related Works In summary, in the domain of cardiac EP, mechanistic DTs are in the most mature stage of developments towards clinical translation, where most are optimized per instance and thus not suitable for rapid adaptation to live data [ 14 , 6 , 38 , 17 ] . While neural and physics-informed alternatives are being actively pursued as efficient surrogates for mechanistic DTs, they are either not adaptive to patient-specific data [ 20 , 28 , 54 , 29 , 25 ] or need to be optimized per instance [ 29 , 38 , 52 ] . Although amortized inference strategies exist for such adaptations, they are not yet adopted for DTs of cardiac EPs and are fundamentally still based on a reconstruction objective. Table. 1 presents a structured summary of existing DT formulations and optimizations strategies, along with their representative works that will be considered as baselines in our experimental studies. Table 1 : Taxonomy of predictive cardiac EP digital-twin (DT) formulations and adaptation strategies. Only works that infer or adapt a reusable forward model for patient-specific prediction are included. Works focused on initial construction of either mechanistic or neural DTs are not included. Model class Per-instance inference (iterative) Amortized inference (learned) White-box (mechanistic) Iterative calibration of patient-specific physical parameters in mechanistic forward models. [ 14 , 6 , 17 , 44 , 32 , 2 , 53 ] Encoder-style amortized adaptation of mechanistic DT parameters; not yet seen in cardiac EP but exist in other physics systems [ 49 ] Gray-box (PINN or hybrid) Iterative personalization of network and/or physics parameters under data-fitting and PDE constraints. [ 16 , 50 ] Encoder-style amortized adaptation of parameters for both neural and mechanistic components; progress limited in cardiac EP [ 43 ] Black-box (neural surrogate) Iterative calibration of input mechanistic parameters to a pre-trained neural-network surrogate [ 29 ] Encoder-style amortized adaptation of parameters for neural surrogates; progress limited in cardiac EP [ 19 ] 2.3 Existing Mechanistic-Neural Hybrid Models Both within and outside the domain of building DTs for cardiac EP, there has been growing interest in hybridizing physics-based models with neural augmentations [ 15 , 34 , 52 ] . For instance, APHYNITY [ 52 ] augments an incomplete physics-based dynamical model with a neural residual term: to avoid the expressive neural component overpowers the physics-based component, it explicitly encourages the physics-based component to explain as much of the dynamics as possible and restricts the neural term to capture only the remaining discrepancy. Similarly, universal differential equations (UDEs) [ 34 ] are presented as a general way to incorporate a neural network within physics-based differential equations, while NeuralSim [ 15 ] provides a broader hybrid-modeling framework in which neural modules are embedded inside differentiable simulators beyond additive formulations. All of these works, however, assume direct supervision on the state variables being modeled by the hybrid models. Furthermore, these hybrid models are commonly fit to observed trajectories via gradient-based system identification, often in a per-instance manner [ 52 ] . Whether the mechanistic and neural components can be separately identified when given indirect observations, and to what extent this may affect the predictive performance of the optimized hybrid model, have not been well investigated. Amortized inference strategies have emerged for optimizing hybrid models. Hybrid-VAE [ 41 ] combines mechanistic dynamics with latent neural components and use an encoder to infer the latent parameters for both components from indirect observations. Robust hybrid learning [ 46 ] improves this by generating expert-augmented training examples using the approximate mechanistic component. These works have relaxed the assumption of direct supervision, i.e., latent parameters of the hybrid model are inferred from indirect observations of the variables the hybrid model describes. They also start to focus on the challenges in separately identifying the physics-based and neural components of the model, although from the lens of ensuring the physics-based component to not be dominated by the expressive neural component. The amortized inference fundamentally still relies on a reconstructive objective, and its impact on the identifiability of the hybrid model are not discussed in these existing works. In a recent work, we have examined the issue of the non-identifiability of naive hybrid deep generative models under indirect observations, and established the first theoretical foundation for leveraging meta-learning as a remedy to address the issue of identifiability [ 51 ] . While focused on theoretical analysis and benchmarked on simple physics systems (such as pendulums), this works lays the theoretical basis for HAPI-EP, which we extend to identifiability-aware hybrid-DTs for cardiac EP that is adaptive and predictive. 3 Methodology We present HAPI-EP by its four main elements: the adaptive and hybrid formulation of the generative DT (Section 3.1 ), its identifiable formulation by conditioning on context samples (Section 3.2 ), the corresponding learn-to-predict inference strategy (Section 3.3 ), and the conditions under which the identifiability of the hybrid DT can be established (Section 3.4 ). 3.1 Hybrid and Adaptive Modeling of Cardi